---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Receptor from "../components/Receptor.astro";
import { GROUP_PAGE, people, research, scales, scaleNames } from "../data/site";

const sequence = [
  { aa: "I", pos: "3.46" },
  { aa: "A", pos: "3.47" },
  { aa: "V", pos: "3.48" },
  { aa: "D", pos: "3.49" },
  { aa: "C", pos: "R3.50C", swap: true },
  { aa: "Y", pos: "3.51" },
  { aa: "L", pos: "3.52" },
  { aa: "A", pos: "3.53" },
];
---

<Base>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <div class="sky dots b">
      <div class="win w1">
        <div class="win-bar">
          mission.txt<span class="stripes"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="win-body">
          We integrate genomics, structural biology, pharmacology and
          health-care data to find new drug targets.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="win w2">
        <div class="win-bar">
          fact<span class="stripes"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="win-body">
          <b>34%</b>of approved drugs act on a G protein-coupled receptor.
        </div>
      </div>
      <Receptor />
      <h1>Every Genome Reads The Same Drug Differently</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="cap accent">
      Pharmacoinformatics, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology,
      University of Copenhagen
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap mission" id="mission">
    <p class="cap">Mission</p>
    <p class="say">
      We integrate large biomedical data in genomics, structural biology,
      pharmacology and health care with new computational methods,{" "}
      <span>
        to find novel drug targets and reveal the principles that run biological
        systems.
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap scales">
    <div class="head">
      <h2>From One Residue To One Patient</h2>
      <p>
        One swapped amino acid can change how well a drug works, or which
        pathway it switches on. We follow that change through four scales.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="seq"
      aria-label="Illustrative stretch of helix 3 with one residue replaced by a variant"
    >
      {sequence.map((r) => (
        <div class:list={["res", { swap: r.swap }]}>
          <b>{r.aa}</b>
          <small>{r.pos}</small>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="cap seq-cap">
      Helix 3 around the DRY motif. Illustrative sequence.
    </p>
    <div class="wins">
      {scales.map((s) => (
        <div class:list={["win", s.color]}>
          <div class="win-bar">
            {s.id}
            <i />
          </div>
          <div class="win-body">
            {s.text}
            <span>{s.tools}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="research" id="research">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <h2>Three Lines Of Work</h2>
        <p>Every project mixes computation and the wet lab.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="works">
        {research.map((w) => (
          <article class="work">
            <div class:list={["tile", "dots", w.color]}>
              <i class="obj" style={w.shape} />
            </div>
            <h3>{w.title}</h3>
            <p>{w.text}</p>
            <div class="reach">
              {scaleNames.map((name) => (
                <span class:list={{ on: w.reach.includes(name) }}>{name}</span>
              ))}
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap software" id="software">
    <div class="head">
      <h2>Software</h2>
      <p>Open-source tools we build and use in our own analyses.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tools">
      <article class="tool">
        <div class="tile dots b">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 320 240"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Dose–response curve fitted through data points"
          >
            <path
              d="M30 20 V210 H305"
              fill="none"
              stroke="#17171A"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            ></path>
            <path
              d="M30 196 C100 196 140 180 165 120 S230 44 300 44"
              fill="none"
              stroke="#17171A"
              stroke-width="3"
            ></path>
            <g fill="#1E4FC2" stroke="#17171A" stroke-width="1.5">
              <circle cx="45" cy="192" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="75" cy="188" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="105" cy="183" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="135" cy="162" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="165" cy="118" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="195" cy="78" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="225" cy="58" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="255" cy="49" r="6"></circle>
              <circle cx="285" cy="46" r="6"></circle>
            </g>
            <g
              font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace"
              font-size="11"
              fill="#6A6A70"
            >
              <text x="305" y="230" text-anchor="end">
                log [drug]
              </text>
              <text x="36" y="16">
                response
              </text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-text">
          <p class="cap">Open source · Rust</p>
          <h3>CurveBook</h3>
          <p>
            Biostatistics, nonlinear curve fitting and scientific graphs,
            written from scratch in Rust. Data, analyses, graphs, layouts and
            notes stay together in one <code>.curvebook</code> file you can send
            to a collaborator.
          </p>
          <div class="facts">
            <span>alpha</span>
            <span>MCP + command line</span>
            <span>GUI in development</span>
          </div>
          <a class="btn lg" href="https://hausergroup.github.io/curvebook/">
            Visit CurveBook{" "}
            <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">
              ↗
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tool">
        <div class="tile dots y">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 320 240"
            role="img"
            aria-label="OPLS-DA score plot with two groups separated along the predictive component"
          >
            <path
              d="M160 20 V220 M20 120 H300"
              fill="none"
              stroke="#17171A"
              stroke-width="1.5"
              stroke-dasharray="4 5"
            ></path>
            <g fill="#fff" stroke="#17171A" stroke-width="2">
              <circle cx="80" cy="90" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="98" cy="140" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="70" cy="125" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="110" cy="105" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="92" cy="170" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="65" cy="75" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="118" cy="152" r="7"></circle>
              <circle cx="86" cy="112" r="7"></circle>
            </g>
            <g fill="#F6C51B" stroke="#17171A" stroke-width="2">
              <rect x="203" y="73" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="225" y="128" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="238" y="93" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="213" y="153" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="191" y="111" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="248" y="138" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="231" y="63" width="14" height="14"></rect>
              <rect x="208" y="98" width="14" height="14"></rect>
            </g>
            <g
              font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace"
              font-size="11"
              fill="#6A6A70"
            >
              <text x="305" y="236" text-anchor="end">
                t[1] predictive
              </text>
              <text x="166" y="16">
                to[1] orthogonal
              </text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="tool-text">
          <p class="cap">Open source · Python</p>
          <h3>scikit-opls</h3>
          <p>
            OPLS and OPLS-DA with a scikit-learn interface. It splits the
            variation in X into a part that predicts y and an orthogonal part
            that doesn't, then fits PLS on the cleaned data. Includes O2PLS, VIP
            scores, S-plots and permutation tests, and works inside{" "}
            <code>Pipeline</code> and <code>GridSearchCV</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="facts">
            <span>v0.2.0</span>
            <span>OPLS · OPLS-DA · O2PLS</span>
            <span>scikit-learn compatible</span>
          </div>
          <a class="btn lg y" href="https://hausergroup.github.io/scikit-opls/">
            Visit scikit-opls{" "}
            <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">
              ↗
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap people" id="people">
    <div class="head">
      <h2>People</h2>
      <p>Computational biologists, pharmacologists and data scientists.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crew">
      {people.map((p) => (
        <article class="person">
          <div class:list={["face", "dots", p.color]} aria-hidden="true">
            <b>{p.initials}</b>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="name">{p.name}</p>
            <p class="cap">{p.title}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="join">
    <div class="panel dots y">
      <h2>Work With Us</h2>
      <div class="win">
        <div class="win-bar">
          join.txt<span class="stripes"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="win-body">
          Thesis, PhD or postdoc between computation and the lab? Find the group
          on the department's website.
          <br />
          <br />
          <a class="btn" href={GROUP_PAGE}>
            Group page at UCPH{" "}
            <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">
              ↗
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
